<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1450 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Vaishnava Jana To</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="gu">વૈષ્ણવ જન તો</bdi> / <bdi lang="gu-Latn">Vaiṣṇava jana to</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Gujarati hymn from the 1400s that says a good person feels others' pain and helps them. Gandhi had it sung every day.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Narsinh Mehta</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (morning ragas); the tune now best known by Narayan Khare, 1920s</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Gujarati Vaishnava Hindus; later Gandhi's ashrams and the Indian independence movement</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Junagadh, Gujarat, India</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>15th century (earliest surviving manuscript 1835; an early 18th-century one reported)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Narsinh Mehta (c. 1414–1480))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Vaishnava Jana To&quot; is a Gujarati devotional poem, a pad, by the 15th-century poet-saint Narsinh Mehta of Junagadh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. Mehta (c. 1414–1480) came from the Nagar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> community, who mostly worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, but he was devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> from childhood and spent much of it singing with sadhus camped outside the town; his devotion kept his family poor. He wrote in the Gujarati that people of the region spoke rather than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, without the backing of any religious order, and from the late 19th century Gujarati scholars called him ādikavi, the first poet, since his songs were the earliest to circulate widely among Gujarati speakers. The pad is a short lyric built on a refrain that a group sings back.</p><p>Its authorship has been studied closely, because the poem is missing from the earliest 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> collections of Mehta's lyrics. The oldest known manuscript that contains it, at the B. J. Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>, is dated 1835 (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikram-samvat">Vikram Samvat</a> 1892), though K. K. Shastri reported examining an early 18th-century one. A 19th-century claim that the poet Dalpatram (1820–1898) wrote it was dismissed once the date of that manuscript was set against the start of his career. Some scholars name Mehta and the 15th century without qualification; others note that the earliest manuscript is of the 18th century. Mehta's poems were first printed in 1852, and this one appeared again in anthologies of 1885 and 1913.</p><p>The poem defines a Vaishnava, a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, by conduct alone: empathy for the suffering of others joined to the duty to relieve it without pride, equal regard for all, truthfulness, restraint, freedom from greed and lust, and steady devotion to God, with nothing said of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, community or gender. Mehta's songs were made for singing to morning ragas, and they are sung on dawn processions through towns and villages (prabhāt-pherī) and in all-night sessions that end at daybreak.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> made the poem a centre of his spiritual practice. It was sung daily at his ashrams, its opening stanza stood on the title page of his Ashram Bhajanavali, and he said that even if he forgot the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a> this hymn alone would sustain him. It was sung on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-march">Salt March</a> in 1930, during his fasts, at his son Devdas's wedding in 1933 and at Kasturba Gandhi's cremation in 1944. When the Christian Joseph Royeppen and a Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> asked to sing &quot;Christian&quot; or &quot;Muslim&quot; in place of &quot;Vaishnava&quot;, Gandhi took it as proof that the poem spoke to everyone. Facing resistance to his campaign for the acceptance of Dalits in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> society, he cited it in his weekly Navajivan: a religion that had produced a devotee who saw all as equal could not hold some people untouchable. In his ashrams, where it was sung daily, Dalit families lived as equals with everyone else.</p><p>In the 1920s Narayan Khare, musician at the Sabarmati ashram, set the poem to a semi-classical tune in the evening raga Mishra Khamaj, to carry it beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>; this, not the old morning tune, is the melody by which it is now known. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> radio played it in the 1940s, often sung by his daughter Mathuriben Khare; Vijay Bhatt's film Narsi Bhagat (1940), made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s suggestion, ends with it; and M. S. Subbulakshmi sang it near the close of her Carnatic concerts. It closes Richard Attenborough's film Gandhi, was sung at Drew Gilpin Faust's installation as Harvard's first woman president in 2007, and was carried by Indian and Pakistani activists on a Dandi March to Gaza in 2010–2011. It has also been sung as a qawwali and used in hip-hop.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The poem answers one question: what makes a true devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>? Its answer is a way of living rather than rites or birth: feeling and easing the suffering of others, treating all alike, speaking truth, wanting nothing that belongs to another, and staying humble and devoted. Its tone is calm and teaching, and it makes goodness, not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, the measure of faith.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Gujarati</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Gujarati</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gu">વૈષ્ણવજન તો તેને રે કહીયે, જે પીડ પરાઈ જાણે રે.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="gu-Latn">vaiṣṇavajana to tene re kahīye, je pīḍa parāī jāṇe re.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Call him a man of Viṣṇu who knows the pain of others.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gu">પરદુઃખે ઉપકાર કરે તોયે, મન અભિમાન ન આણે રે. — વૈષ્ણવ ૧.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="gu-Latn">paraduḥkhe upakāra kare toye, mana abhimāna na āṇe re. — vaiṣṇava 1.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He does good to others in their sorrow, and even so lets no pride come into his mind. — Vaiṣṇava 1.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gu">સકળ લોકમાં સહુને વંદે, નિંદા ન કરે કેની રે.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="gu-Latn">sakaḷa lokamāṁ sahune vaṁde, niṁdā na kare kenī re.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In all the world he bows to everyone; he speaks ill of no one.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gu">વાચ કાછ મન નિશ્ચલ રાખે, ધન ધન જનની તેની રે. — વૈષ્ણવ ૨.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="gu-Latn">vāca kācha mana niścala rākhe, dhana dhana jananī tenī re. — vaiṣṇava 2.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He keeps speech, loins and mind steady: blessed, blessed is his mother. — Vaiṣṇava 2.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gu">સમદ્રષ્ટિને તૃષ્ણા ત્યાગી, પરસ્ત્રી જેને માત રે.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="gu-Latn">samadraṣṭine tr̥ṣṇā tyāgī, parastrī jene māta re.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He looks on all with an equal eye and has given up craving; another's wife is his mother.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gu">જિહ્વા થકી અસત્ય ન બોલે, પરધન નવ ઝાલે હાથ રે.— વૈષ્ણવ ૩.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="gu-Latn">jihvā thakī asatya na bole, paradhana nava jhāle hātha re.— vaiṣṇava 3.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>His tongue speaks no untruth; he lays no hand on another's wealth. — Vaiṣṇava 3.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gu">મોહ માયા વ્યાપે નહિ જેને, દ્રઢ વૈરાગ્ય જેના મનમાં રે.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="gu-Latn">moha māyā vyāpe nahi jene, draḍha vairāgya jenā manamāṁ re.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Delusion and illusion do not take hold of him; firm detachment is in his mind.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gu">રામ નામ શું તાળી રે વાગી, સકળ તિરથ તેના તનમાં રે. — વૈષ્ણવ ૪.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="gu-Latn">rāma nāma śuṁ tāḷī re vāgī, sakaḷa tiratha tenā tanamāṁ re. — vaiṣṇava 4.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>His clap has struck to the name of Rāma; all the places of pilgrimage are in his body. — Vaiṣṇava 4.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gu">વણલોભી ને કપટ રહિત છે, કામ ક્રોધ નિવાર્યા રે.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="gu-Latn">vaṇalobhī ne kapaṭa rahita che, kāma krodha nivāryā re.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He is without greed and without deceit; he has held off desire and anger.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gu">ભણે નરસૈયો તેનું દર્શન કરતા, કુળ ઈકોતેર તાર્યા રે. — વૈષ્ણવ ૫.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="gu-Latn">bhaṇe narasaiyo tenuṁ darśana karatā, kuḷa īkotera tāryā re. — vaiṣṇava 5.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Says Narsaiyo: in seeing him, seventy-one generations of the family were carried across. — Vaiṣṇava 5.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Attributed to Narsinh Mehta (15th century). Text from <a href="https://gu.wikisource.org/wiki/%E0%AA%B5%E0%AB%88%E0%AA%B7%E0%AB%8D%E0%AA%A3%E0%AA%B5%E0%AA%9C%E0%AA%A8_%E0%AA%A4%E0%AB%8B_%E0%AA%A4%E0%AB%87%E0%AA%A8%E0%AB%87_%E0%AA%B0%E0%AB%87_%E0%AA%95%E0%AA%B9%E0%AB%80%E0%AA%AF%E0%AB%87" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ISO 15919).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: ISO 15919, letter for letter, so the inherent a is written even where Gujarati does not sound it (vaiṣṇavajana, pīḍa, māta, hātha, tiratha are said vaiṣṇavjan, pīḍ, māt, hāth, tirath); anusvara ં is ṁ (lokamāṁ, vaṁde, śuṁ), ળ is ḷ, ઋ-sign r̥; e and o without macrons (Gujarati has no short/long pair). Digits of the refrain numbers as 1-5. 're' is a filler/emphatic particle of the song, romanized and not translated.</li><li>refrain '— vaiṣṇava n.' is the repeat sign of the original (sing the first line again); kept as it stands.</li><li>line 2.2: vāca kācha mana, 'speech, kācha, mind'. kācha (Hindi kāchh) is the tucked-in end of the loincloth, standing for sexual restraint; 'loins' keeps the image. Some read it as 'deed' or 'body'.</li><li>line 3.1: the text writes samadraṣṭine as one word; it is samadraṣṭi ne 'equal-sighted and' (ne = 'and' here).</li><li>line 4.2: 'rāma nāma śuṁ tāḷī vāgī', literally 'a clap (tāḷī) has sounded with the name of Rāma': an idiom for being wholly attuned to, absorbed in, the name (as one claps in time to a hymn).</li><li>line 4.1: moha and māyā, 'infatuation / delusion' and 'illusion / worldly attachment'.</li><li>line 5.2: Narsaiyo is the poet Narsinh Mehta's signature name (the bhaṇe 'says' formula). 'kuḷa īkotera', seventy-one generations of a family, saved (carried across the ocean of rebirth); whose family is not said: the seer's, by the usual reading.</li><li>Gujarati is translated plainly; 'a man of Viṣṇu' renders vaiṣṇavajana (a devotee of Viṣṇu). The dropped spaces and punctuation of the text are kept in rom ('hātha re.— vaiṣṇava 3.').</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
